{"name":"Rheingold","hex":"#fadca7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Rheingold","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A treasure of gold that lies at the bottom of the Rhine, guarded by the three Rhine maidens  and afterward owned by the Nibelungs and Siegfried."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from German."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Rheingold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Das Rheingold","blocks":[{"text":"Das Rheingold, WWV 86A, is the first of the four epic music dramas that constitute Richard Wagner's cycle Der Ring des Nibelungen. It premiered as a single opera at the National Theatre of Munich on 22 September 1869, and received its first performance as part of the Ring cycle at the Bayreuth Festspielhaus on 13 August 1876."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/1876Rhinemaidens.jpg/330px-1876Rhinemaidens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Das_Rheingold"},{"title":"Rheingold (train)","blocks":[{"text":"The Rheingold ('Rhinegold') was a named train that operated between Hook of Holland, near Rotterdam, and Geneva, Switzerland, a distance of 1,067 kilometres (663 mi), until 1987. Another section of the train started in Amsterdam and was coupled to the Hoek cars in Utrecht. The Rheingold ran along the Rhine River via Arnhem, Netherlands, and Cologne, Germany, using special luxury coaches. It was named after Richard Wagner's Das Rheingold opera, which romanticized the Rhine. From 1965 until the train's discontinuation in 1987, the Rheingold was a first-class-only Trans Europ Express (TEE) train."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Rheingold%2C_1928%2C_Friese.jpg/330px-Rheingold%2C_1928%2C_Friese.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheingold_(train)"},{"title":"Rheingold (surname)","blocks":[{"text":"Rheingold is a surname, meaning \"gold of the Rhine river\"."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheingold_(surname)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Walhalla_%281896%29_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg/500px-Walhalla_%281896%29_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Walhalla_%281896%29_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg/500px-Walhalla_%281896%29_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wotan's fortress of Valhalla (Brückner, 1896)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalhalla_(1896)_by_Max_Br%C3%BCckner.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Ring11.jpg/500px-Ring11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Ring11.jpg/500px-Ring11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Alberich and the subjugated Nibelung dwarfs (illustration by Arthur Rackham, 1910)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARing11.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Ed0002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Ed0002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Wotan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEd0002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/FischerRheingold.jpg/500px-FischerRheingold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/FischerRheingold.jpg/500px-FischerRheingold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emil Fischer as Wotan at the 1889 New York premiere","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFischerRheingold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg/500px-Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg/1280px-Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg/500px-Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The gods begin to lose their youth on Freia's departure","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_034.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10450%2C_Rheingold-Express%2C_Salon-Wagen_I._Klasse.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10450%2C_Rheingold-Express%2C_Salon-Wagen_I._Klasse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10450%2C_Rheingold-Express%2C_Salon-Wagen_I._Klasse.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10450%2C_Rheingold-Express%2C_Salon-Wagen_I._Klasse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Interior of a first class coach belonging to the Rheingold in September 1930","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABundesarchiv_Bild_102-10450%2C_Rheingold-Express%2C_Salon-Wagen_I._Klasse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Aussichtswagen_innen.jpg/500px-Aussichtswagen_innen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Aussic